## What landed (`a971bfc3`)

The fifth verdict. USAGE_ABSENT was absorbing two opposite facts about a session. Split into:

• `USAGE_ABSENT` — no records at all (file empty/absent): checked, holds nothing
• `USAGE_NO_TERMINAL_EMIT — records **present**, no usage line: the process was killed before emit_output`, the one thing that writes the usage line

Four sites, not one — the task named the classifier, but the landing zone is the constant, classify_session_usage, the fold usage_coverage, and render_usage_coverage. Two things had to move with it:

• **The fold's bare else is gone.** A new verdict landing there renders as *"I could not read the file"*. An unrecognised verdict now raises instead of being filed under corruption — the one direction where loud beats quiet.
• **apply_usage_boundary demotes the new state too, as a stated decision.** "A file that exists is post-boundary by construction" was the tempting reading; measured false — 489 of 624 live session dirs are records-with-no-usage and every one predates 8a633cff. Leaving it undemoted re-runs the Day-181 false alarm 489 times.
• **In the render it's part of the healthy test, not only the alarm path.** A killed-only window has `absent == 0 and unreadable == 0`, so without adding it to that conjunction the line would say *"#848 channel is live"* over a window where every expensive run died.

Both directions in one table: records-with-no-usage → NO_TERMINAL_EMIT beside empty → ABSENT, each row carrying its record/usage counts, plus an anti-vacuous assertion that the two rows really do supply different inputs.

